      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks, that makes sense. i missed the ownership issue with the sensor packet, that's definitely a huge leak. \n\nyou're right about the error path too. i'll switch to using smart pointers or a pre-allocated buffer to avoid that manual management. \n\naside from the obvious code errors, what are some ways i can monitor the heap in real-time on the hardware itself? like, if i can't run heavy tools like valgrind, how do i actually \"see\" these leaks happening as they occur so i can correlate them with specific sensor inputs or system states? i need to be able to track the \"drift\" in memory usage without crashing the system.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The core technique is **heap instrumentation via allocator hooks** — you intercept every allocation and track it. It's lightweight enough to run on target. ## Technique 1: Global new/delete Override with a Live Allocation Registry ```cpp struct AllocInfo { void* ptr; size_t size; const char* tag; }; // Lock-free ring buffer or mutex-protected table AllocInfo g_alloc_table[MAX_TRACKED]; void* operator new(size_t size) { void* p = malloc(size); track_allocation(p, size); // record ptr+size return p; } void operator delete(void* p) noexcept { untrack_allocation(p); // remove record free(p); } ``` Now you can expose `live_count`, `live_bytes`, `peak_bytes` as telemetry channels. Plot `live_bytes` over time — a sawtooth that trends upward in a sawtooth pattern that trends upward = leak. What makes this powerful for **correlation**: extend `track_allocation` to also capture a **tag/context ID** — current sensor ID, state machine state, message type — when the allocation happens. Then when `live_bytes` drifts up, you can query: > \"Which tags are accumulating?\" If tag == `SENSOR_IL`",
      "source": "target",
